WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.980
How many online tools hold our company
data, and how many can IT name?

00:00:05.265 --> 00:00:08.515
If the second number is smaller,
that gap is the risk.

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:10.310
It opens in four ways.

00:00:11.683 --> 00:00:14.293
First, a team signs up with a credit card.

00:00:14.553 --> 00:00:19.183
Sales finds a better lead tool, and
uploads the customer list to get started.

00:00:19.478 --> 00:00:22.948
Nobody asks IT, because asking IT is slow.

00:00:23.273 --> 00:00:27.603
We now store customer data with a
supplier we have no contract with.

00:00:27.883 --> 00:00:30.733
Under GDPR, we are still responsible.

00:00:31.883 --> 00:00:34.533
Second, work happens on personal accounts.

00:00:34.788 --> 00:00:37.638
A key presentation is
finished on a home laptop.

00:00:38.048 --> 00:00:40.638
A contract is forwarded
to a personal mailbox.

00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:45.823
There is no second factor, no backup,
and no way to wipe a lost device.

00:00:46.193 --> 00:00:48.803
When that person leaves,
the data leaves too.

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:52.280
Third, the subscription nobody remembers.

00:00:52.695 --> 00:00:57.485
A pilot that never became a project
still holds the data that was uploaded.

00:00:57.795 --> 00:00:59.785
It still bills the card every month.

00:01:00.075 --> 00:01:03.545
And its administrator may have
left the company two years ago.

00:01:04.483 --> 00:01:07.333
Fourth, one click hands an
app the keys to the mailbox.

00:01:07.893 --> 00:01:10.423
A scheduling assistant
asks to read the calendar,

00:01:10.953 --> 00:01:14.003
and in the same dialog,
all mail and all files.

00:01:14.483 --> 00:01:15.933
The employee clicks Allow.

00:01:16.298 --> 00:01:21.268
That access survives a password reset,
and is not stopped by the second factor.

00:01:22.283 --> 00:01:23.493
What good looks like.

00:01:23.778 --> 00:01:27.228
Single sign-on makes the
company login the only door.

00:01:27.493 --> 00:01:32.943
Discovery from expense reports and login
logs shows which tools are really in use.

00:01:33.273 --> 00:01:37.923
Admin consent means employees cannot
grant mail access on their own.

00:01:38.123 --> 00:01:41.843
NIS2 asks for an inventory
of assets and suppliers,

00:01:41.843 --> 00:01:44.913
and security across the supply chain.

00:01:46.597 --> 00:01:47.807
A suggested first step.

00:01:48.092 --> 00:01:51.522
Ask how many online tools
hold company data today.

00:01:51.777 --> 00:01:54.207
Then ask how many of them IT can name.

00:01:54.472 --> 00:01:57.642
One supplier register,
with an owner for each tool.

00:01:57.892 --> 00:01:59.282
No owner, no tool.

00:02:00.883 --> 00:02:03.713
You cannot protect data
you do not know you have.

00:02:04.028 --> 00:02:05.358
Bring it back into view.

